  ('553e7b9d-88e0-5776-9395-cd16704246ec', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The suffix -ous means "full of" or "having," and danger means "a chance of harm." Why does the passage say thick ash is "dangerous" to breathe?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Breathing it is safe as long as you stay calm","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"It is full of smells that people do not like","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-base-word"},{"text":"Breathing it has a strong chance of causing harm","is_correct":true},{"text":"It makes the air too cold to breathe outside","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Breathing it has a strong chance of causing harm$q$, $q$Dangerous means "full of danger," so breathing thick ash can harm people. A bad smell is not the same as danger, and the passage never says ash smells.$q$, 'draft', '17773410-8578-588b-a135-7ef47bcf2369')
